A||N||34||trigger||kit cars are a waste||||1||
Z||000000||trigger||10-28-2000||08:50 PM||||Stimpy here's the topic I promised you.  I think a few body mods are ok.  However, there is no need to waste a Fiero no matter how bad it is.  If you're going to stretch the frame, just build on from scratch.  Kit cars a waste of Fieros.  They are different enough, and there aren't enough of them to change.<P>------------------<BR>Honestly officer, I was just keeping up with the flow of traffic.  I know there aren't any cars in the area, that's how far behind I am.||||reg||1
Z||000001||stimpy||10-28-2000||11:40 PM||||Oooh, contraversy!  <BR>I like it. I've always felt with kit fieros with stock engines that people were throwing away the wrong part of the Fiero.  I mean, I love the looks of the Finale as much as the next guy, who really is fooled by 98% of the conversions out there? <BR>Besides that, Kit Car magazines SUCK! Have you noticed page after page after page of Cobra crap, every so often a Fiero based kit is thrown in. And most of the time they talk up that "Lotus-based suspension" as been one of the main advantages of tearing apart a Fiero.  Sad really... ||||reg||1
Z||000002||007DOUG||10-28-2000||11:44 PM||||I'll be the first to disagree.<P>How else can I get the look of a $250,000 car.<P>AND still afford the insurance.<P>AND get better than 10 miles per gallon.<P>AND still work on it myself rather than pay $120+ hour to the mechanic. <P>AND avoid the pain in the neck (and wallet) of a full tune-up every 10K miles.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||10-29-2000||12:05 AM||||I see kit cars on the same level as ricers.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||10-29-2000||12:35 AM||||I tend to view kit cars like a F355 as being different than a rebody like the Finale. One is a copy of an expensive supercar, the other one is an original body design.<P>I've seen some nice kits and I've seen some pretty lame ones... when you see a beautiful ferrari kit with a stock Fiero engine in the back though, it sorta spoils the effect....<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||4
Z||000005||rockcrawl||10-29-2000||02:00 PM||||OK, Here I go!,<BR>I spent $2500 to restore an '86 Fiero that I bought from the salvage yard. I saved it from the crusher. After trying to sell it for two years, I finally got $900 for it.<P>I spent $12000 to build a V8 Countach replica on a $100 stripped Fiero. I sold it two days after I posted an ad on the net for $30000. <P>It is because of Kitcars that I have come to admire the Fiero. Before I got the idea to build a kitcar, I was a Fiero hater like the other 99% of the worlds population. I have owned at least 12 Fieros. I just bought another one last Friday. I have pulled two of them from salvage yards and put them back on the road. I have almost all of the old Fiero magazine ads framed and hanging on my wall.<P>To say that Kitcars are a waste is just ignorant! In my opinion restoring a 2M4 is a waste! Most of the guys that are building kitcars are not starting with a mint '88 GT, they are using cars that were headed to the crusher anyway. Any useable Fiero parts are stripped off and sold to keep other Fieros (like yours) running. Without kitcars, companies (and products) like RCC, Ryane, and probably even V8 Archie wouldn't be around.<P>Not everyone has the same priorities. Not everyone wants to do 13sec in the 1/4 mile. Lots of people are happy to just drive through town at 35 and see everyone take a second look. That's why you see exotic replicas with 4 cyl engines. I don't see a problem with that, after all, the Fiero came with a 4 cyl and a racey body. Lighten up, and try to realize that everyone has their own opinions and ideas. This topic has come up before, and I'm sure it will come up again. The Fiero is not as rare as you think.<P>If you'd like to see my waste of a Fiero that made me $18000 profit, click the link  <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/lambo.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/lambo.html</A> <P>Have a nice day!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||7
Z||000006||2M4 Dale||10-29-2000||02:27 PM||||While I like...no Love... the look and styling of the Pontiac Fiero it's not the only car I like. I have a fascination with British and Italian sports cars and I have to admit that I even like a few of the Japanese sports cars. I have driven GM all my life with only a few exceptions. I was raised on Pontiacs (Cats, Bonneys, and Goats). My father and uncle loved 'em too. I really have an affinity for them. The fact remains I will never be able to afford a Ferrari or Lambo or Ford GT40 without hittin' the lottery. I could never stand to see someone put a Chevelle body on a Bazer frame...yuk...or things like that. But a well done kit shows that the owner / builder has taken some initiative to do something himself/herself...lol. I don't have the time or money at this time to start a kit project but when my barn/garage is finished...well we'll see.<P>What better base for a dream car than a Fiero with it's own unique drivability and fun factor built right in.||||reg||7
Z||000007||stimpy||10-29-2000||02:53 PM||||Aaah, love the sound of divergent opinions...<BR>Rockcrawl, I have checked out your website in the past, and I think you do a hellacious job making the most out of the Fiero package.  Wasn't your Lambo in a Kit magazine lately? Somehow I recall seeing it. And I gotta admit, I would not turn down a 355 replica should someone hand me the keys.<BR>  My point is this, with my (meager) resources, I would prefer to use the aftermarket to pump up the guts of my Fiero, improve upon the powertrain suspension, and brakes, and also bump up the creature comforts.  Now a Lambo replica, that's a real commitment. But I gotta say a Ferrarri replica with a stock interior and a 4 banger ain't gona fool anyone.  I would prefer to see something very different like the Finale then something that tries to ape the look of another car like, well, all of PISA's kits. <P>One other kit that would sway me, I think, would be the Aldino K/O. While it does imitate the BB512, it is in the Koenig style. One of those bad boys with a LT-1 would be pretty darn cool.  Too much bucks for me. I guess it all comes down to sour grapes in my case.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||10-29-2000||05:31 PM||||Stimpy,<BR> I don't think that was my car in the magazine. Well, I know it wasn't, because it's not my car anymore, but maybe the new owner got it in a mag. I haven't seen it. The Aldino K/O is a cool car, but the only one I've ever seen had a Fiero interior and a 2.8.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
Z||000009||fierodave||10-29-2000||05:48 PM||||Rockcrawl~~~~very well put!<BR>FD<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://members.theglobe.com/fierodave/gifs/fierosig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Spyder||10-29-2000||06:05 PM||||Them kit car guys, <BR>Bastards!<BR>LOL<BR>We’ve had this discussion before so I aint sayin nothin. <A HREF="http://www.markalexandermiller.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.markalexandermiller.com</A> <BR>||||reg||12
Z||000011||Bandit2_0||10-29-2000||07:54 PM||||I would have to agree with Trigger up there about the fact that their aren't that many Fiero's around.  The only problem is that if you build a kit car to look like a F355, the car can only look as cool as the guy in it.<P>Bandit<P>------------------<BR>He's the kind of guy that would drink a gallon of gas just so he could piss on your campfire.||||reg||6
Z||000012||286SE'S||10-30-2000||11:53 AM||||I don't think a business like Mr. Mikes Leather would be around if it wasnt for<BR>the Kit cars that Fieros are made into.<BR>After you spend 15,000 for a kit you want<BR>your interior to be 'special' too. He doesn't make seats for Hondas but if he did would<BR>be swamped with orders? Be thankfull for these little companies that make cool stuff just for Fieros.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Tigger||10-30-2000||01:04 PM||||Oh boy, Kit Bashing time!<P>I think the decision on whether or not to kit out a Fiero is up to the individual owner and not the government.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Kit cars shouldn't be put on the same level as the cars they were made to look like.  Way I look at it, If you can make a kit indistingishable with the real thing on the outside at 10 feet away, you've did what you set out to do.  Basically, give everyone a thrill at the fraction of the cost.<P>I bet if you took a Fiero and threw $250,000 at a kit, you'd have a custom interior and be cranking a V12, then again I'd be buying the real thing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> j/k||||reg||1
Z||000014||DaRkLoRD||10-30-2000||07:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tigger:<BR><B>I bet if you took a Fiero and threw $250,000 at a kit, you'd have a custom interior and be cranking a V12, then again I'd be buying the real thing.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> j/k</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>For $250,000.... you could build 10 kits.. LOL<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||12
Z||000015||Robert||10-30-2000||08:25 PM||||One the other hand……..<P><BR>I wouldn’t be a Fiero owner today if it weren’t for the Kit Cars.  I originally bought my 87 GT to convert it to a Countach.  But after owning it for a while, I decided to restore it rather than change it.  It may not turn as many heads as a Countach, but it turns enough.<P><P>------------------<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://canterburytrails.com/photo/T2.GIF"> <P> “If you don’t look for that parking space near the entrance, you’ll never get it”||||reg||10
Z||000016||DJRice||10-30-2000||10:44 PM||||Either way, none of us are happy unless we can bust our own knuckles on the car..whether it be swapping the engine, upgrading the suspension, replacing the body panels, or just chaning the oil.  <P>But if I had the money to build a 355 kit and make it look good, I would definitely throw down.  <P><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000017||Fiero5||10-31-2000||09:44 PM||||You know, it's funny. I had a thread similar to this not too long ago, asking when people thought it was OK to part out a Fiero. Quite a few thought that since it was your car, you could do with it what you wanted. Though that is true, it wasn't really where I was coming from. One of the points that I was trying to communicate was the attempt to try and slow down the thinning of the herd so to speak.<P>Here is a thread that comes at the thinning of the herd subject from another angle. I hope this thread doesn't get as heated as mine did at one point.<P>It is a fascinating subject. As the selection of Fieros continually thins out over time, at what point will people start to think about trying to keep what Fieros are left whole cars? It is easy today to say, hey there are plenty out there to do with what anybody wants, but for how much longer? Maybe 5 or 10 years? It may be true that you can still find some awsome buys, but at some point as "arguably" good Fieros are parted out or stripped out for kit cars (not to mention the normal thinning by way of rust, accidents and so forth), the good ones will only become even more difficult to find and afford. Granted parting out is a normal process to keep the rest of the herd going, but some discretion at some point may be needed. <P>I can see a day not too far off when even a fair condition high miles plane jane GT will sell for 6 or 7 grand, as they will become even harder to find do to the above mentioned circumstances.<BR>Hmmm, makes you think, huh?||||reg||1
Z||000018||2xsess||10-31-2000||10:06 PM||||Heres a thought, what if I did a kit that didn't require stretching of the frame and I kept the original body panels that way I could always put it back. <P>What about IMSA fieros? are they considered a wasted Fiero too?or cutting a section out of the firewall to build a sub enclosure?<BR>Isn't that ruining the car too?<P>I'm not for or against kits it's just my angle on the whole thing<P><BR>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT<p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 10-31-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||Black88GT||10-31-2000||10:11 PM||||Gosh he's still stuck on the damn parting out issue.  Give it a break Steve, obviously no one cares.  This is about kit cars, in your own words, <P><B>"Get over it already and let everyone get back to what this post was about"</B>   <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000020||Fiero5||11-01-2000||08:09 AM||||"Gosh he's still stuck on the damn parting out issue."<P>Hey bright lite, what do you think they do with Fieros that are used for kit's??<BR>They part them out! Duh!<BR>I am glad at least one of us is staying topical. I have had quite a few e-mails just this morning since posting that response yesterday, all positive.<P>So Jeff, why don't you go bug somebody else, and stop trying to stir stuff up.||||reg||1
Z||000021||ChadMan||11-01-2000||08:42 AM||||I love Fieros as much as the next guy, but IMHO, people who build kits out of them aren't committing any sin because Fieros simply aren't rare enough to worry about yet - not even the 88's.  If you're worried about the scarcity of Fieros, buy a nice one and preserve it (or a crappy one and restore it).<P>That said, I could never see myself building a Fiero kit car.  I definitely respect the effort that goes into building a nice one (like rogergarrison's 328 replica, for example).  But if I were going to build a kit car, I would want to end up with something faithful to the original car (like the Porsche Speedster kits that are built from a shortened Bug chassis and a hopped-up Beetle engine - not too different from the original Porsche 356).||||reg||1
Z||000022||Tigger||11-01-2000||09:13 AM||||I hate to say it, but more Fieros are probably wiped out in accidents than being made into kits each year.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif"> ||||reg||9
Z||000023||rockcrawl||11-01-2000||03:59 PM||||Good point Tigger. It would be interesting to know exactly how many Fiero's are totalled each year, and also how many are kitted/parted-out. I personally have never totalled a car, but I have parted at least 6 of them including two used for kit cars.<P> I could go out today and find ten Fieros within a 20 mile radius of my home for under $500. It seems to me a little thinning would do us Fiero owners good by increasing the value of the cars we already own. I dream of the day my high milage GT is worth $6-$7000. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||7
Z||000024||william_thorniii||11-01-2000||04:14 PM||||Friends,<P>I dislike getting involved in a conversation of this nature, but I find it amusing that some feel the Fiero is "scarce".  How many members of this Forum are there?  Did anyone else read the recent magazine article about how the Fiero has more national clubs & internet communities than ANY other car? <P>Scarce?  I think not.  Once again let the owners of EACH respective vehicle do as he/she pleases.  The less Fiero's there are, the more the value of mine goes up :~)<P>William T. Thorn, III<BR>AACA Member<P>(Stimpy, you inspire me)||||reg||13
Z||000025||1FST2M6||11-01-2000||04:35 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I LOVE IT!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">    oh my side hurts... HEHE!    <P><BR>"hey everyone cat fight in the girls locker room"    <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 11-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||Fiero5||11-02-2000||12:58 PM||||I myself am not for or against parting out a Fiero, except in unusual circumstances. I personally would love to own a Lamborghini Countach Fiero rebody someday. I am only playing devils advocate and trying to get people to see both sides of the coin for discussion. It's interesting to see the many different points of view.<P>It is true as I have said before, Fieros are not that scarce - yet. Though I would disagree that good clean 88 GT's are easily available to the average Fiero buyer. Also, you have to remember that even though you may be lucky in the fact that where YOU live, there is an apparent ubundance of inexpensive Fieros available. But in other areas, as we have seen many recent posts stating, many do not share that advantage.<P>I would agree that Fieros are lost by accidents way more than for any other reason. Though ones that have been damaged in an accident usually then go to junk yards and places like Kick Hill which are then obviously parted out, as it should be. To just crush a good parts car though, or even a restorable one doesn't make any sense.<P>I remember on one of the Fiero lists awhile back, some listee's were getting upset because someone was finding good buys on nice running Fieros, and just parting them out for the money. Though I would rather turn around and either keep the car, or fix it up if needed and sell it whole, this brought up a variety of feelings and differing of opinions on the subject. Somebody even argued about if you found James Deans original sports car, would you part that out in pieces to make many sales, or sell it whole and get one sale.<P>Lastly, we all know that when you buy a car, you own it so you can do anything you want with it, legally of course :o). That's one of the fun things about owning your own vehicle. But if we all agreed on that fact, we wouldn't keep having all these posts come up to give us something to think about and talk about, now would we?? :o)||||reg||1
Z||000027||Northstar308||11-02-2000||10:01 PM||||I'll have to agree that I think it's a shame to make some of the kits out there that people build.  They look like kits.  Some of the crap kits people build look way worse than a beat up Fiero.  <BR>That having been said, a really nicely done kit has some true advantages.  I am in the process of building a Ferrari 308 Replica at the current moment.  I had actually considered buying a real 308, but didn't feel like paying $5000 for a tune up (no joke).  I could afford the car (*more later*), but wouldn't have enjoyed driving it as much because of the maintenance costs.<BR>As I said, I could afford the real car; A quick search turns up several in super nice, low mileage condition for under $25,000.  Now, it will have cost more to build my replica 308 than that.  A few reasons:<BR>1.  Mine has a 95 Cadillac Northstar engine/trans in it (possible 0-60 in 4.8 bone stock).<BR>2.  I am using a completely new interior (italian wool carpet, leather euro dash (pisa) and door panels, etc., mr Mikes Leather seats).<BR>3.  I am using IRM wheels which look like the real ones (not some of the even cheaper knock offs)<BR>4.  It will be getting a top-notch paint job.<P>These things, combined with a Clarion GPS/MP3/CD/Voice Controlled Stereo System (this thing is really great!!!), the Cadillac guage-pack, and other goodies like the auto-dimming mirror with elec. compass, make my car personalized to my taste.  It will fool 99% of all of you (yes, even you Fiero Lovers) when it is done.  The only people that will know the difference is Ferrari afficianados (even my door handles will be in the right fashion, a pull up design).  It will have a full targa roof, etc.  Enough though (*I'm just really excited as I build it*).<BR>The point is, my car will not only be more reliable and cheaper to maintain than any real 308 out there, it will also kick it's as* on the pavement.  It's got a better low end speed, and a higher top end speed.  All this, and 25mpg on AVERAGE.  <BR>My insurance is cheaper (though a Ferrari doesn't have to be super expensive to insure), I have brand new paint, new wheels/tires, new leather interior, etc. rather than 20 year old pieces.<BR>I commend people out there with the patience to make the Fiero (dare I say?) a better car.  We all know that the Fiero engineers designed the car to take bigger engines, bigger wheels; and prettier bodies.  It just wasn't out there long enough to meet these desires (though the Fastback GT is gorgeous).  <BR>Just my 2 Cents,<P>Josh<P>Oh, and PS, those of you that like us kit builders for part sources, my fastback body panels hit eBay this week  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000028||project74||11-03-2000||01:42 PM||||I'm stripping two daily driver fastback GT's for F355 projects. But I'll keep the cute '86 SE original. Hope that makes everyone happy.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Fiero5||11-03-2000||01:56 PM||||What color are the fastbacks? Are you going to offer the parts for sale?<BR>I could use some fastback sections to convert one of my notchbacks to a fastback :o)<P>------------------<BR>                                                                                                             <IMG SRC="http://freepages.ugo.com/RodeysPlace/files/fieroanim.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000030||rogergarrison||11-04-2000||08:45 AM||||thanks for the complement Chadman. I spent lots of money making my Ferrari kit look as authentic as possible and the performance is up to snuff too,275 hp V6 turbo. When I blow by you at 150 mph on freeway youll never know if its real or not. Ive even had Ferrari dealer in town think my car was authentic when I went up to pick up some stuff. Had to show him enging to prove i wasnt lying. The Ferrari 308/328 is a classic body style, even Ferrari used same one for 20 years, and people still ask me if mine is a new one. Now (my opinion) heres the bad stuff last time I checked, Fieros are not even close to being considered 'classic'. I think the styling of Fiero rates rite up there with AMC Pacer, Gremlin, Festiva. I know you guys love yours and I respect that and I know you put as much work in yours as I do mine. I would never have even considered buying a stock Fiero ever but they are a great platform for a kit. Like was said many times, I got it because its cheap to maintain and It looks like a $150,00.00 car. Of course I never would have chopped up a nice car to make a kit. So look at it as I saved a chassis headed for the crusher. Please dont start flaming me cause I hate the looks of Fiero, Im just answering the kit bashers.<P>Roger<P>ps, the girls dont know its a kit, hehe||||reg||1
Z||000031||William||11-04-2000||06:00 PM||||Where did you locate the 308 kit? I haven't found any available from a US maker.||||reg||1
Z||000032||MikeE||11-04-2000||06:05 PM||||I might as well add my two cents, although it may never be read, being at the bottom of this list.<P>I originally wanted a Fiero because here in Hawaii the salt air attacks every nice car without discrimination and having a Fiero means you can have a nice looking car that stays nice.  Although I like kit cars in general, I have never seen a Fiero rebody that I really liked.  However, I am an oddball in that while I like all Fieros, I like the 2nd gen coupe best and the fastback least, but looks are very subjective.<P>That being said, I agree with Stimpy that for me, the fun of a Fiero is playing around with interesting engine swaps, susp mods and so forth while retaining the stock body and making only minor cosmetic upgrades.  Like DaRkLoRD, I have always been more intriqued with original rebodies than with replicas.  However, I realize that Fieros have poor resale and are really only valuable to a small lunatic fringe, in which I proudly include myself.  Therefore, rockcrawl's post really surprised me.  I had no idea that a Fiero based replica could bring that much money.  I wonder if an original design rebody could do as well?  I doubt it.<P>Because the resale of fieros is low, I will keep a close watch on costs and do only cost effective mods like the Grand Am front brake mod, making my own swap kit, mixing various stock Fiero body parts to get the look I want, and even finding interesting wheels off other GM FWD specialty cars that will look good but give a different look and won't break the bank (like the 16" wheels off the Grand Am SE or those from the various Z34s like the Grand Prix or Lumina).<P>Also, I like the idea that Fieros are being saved from the crusher which is the fate of many Fieros in Hawaii (no space on an island).<p>[This message has been edited by MikeE (edited 11-04-2000).]||||reg||9
Z||000033||pherder||11-04-2000||07:46 PM||||_<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 11-05-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000034||rogergarrison||11-05-2000||12:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by William:<BR><B>Where did you locate the 308 kit? I haven't found any available from a US maker.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Fiero Conversions Inc<BR>3410 Walker Rd<BR>Windsor, Ont, Canada N8W3S3<P>phone (519) 972-4989<P>has both 308 and 328, everything except wheels,emblems,front turn lites, grill.<P>list is $4,500||||reg||1
